@* Freelance. The call to action preselects the contact topic. *@
@inject IContactTopicState TopicState
<section class="section" id="freelance" aria-labelledby="freelance-title">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">03 / Freelance Work</p>
    <h2 id="freelance-title">Collaborate with Me</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Not every problem needs a full-time hire. I take on short, well-defined engagements for teams that want senior .NET, Angular, React, or SQL Server help without the ramp-up, and I keep the first step small so there is nothing to lose by trying it.</p>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <article class="card service-card">
        <div class="service-icon" aria-hidden="true">
          <Icon Name="preview" />
        </div>
        <h3>Low-risk preview</h3>
        <p>Start with one small, fixed-scope piece of work, a day or two at most, so you can see how I communicate, estimate, and deliver before committing to anything bigger.</p>
        <p>If it is not a fit, you still walk away with finished, tested work and a clear write-up of what I found.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card service-card">
        <div class="service-icon" aria-hidden="true">
          <Icon Name="wrench" />
        </div>
        <h3>Small changes</h3>
        <p>Bug fixes, small features, framework and dependency upgrades, and the tickets that never reach the top of the backlog.</p>
        <p>Scoped up front and delivered as clean pull requests against your repository, with tests, so your team can review and merge on its own schedule.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card service-card">
        <div class="service-icon" aria-hidden="true">
          <Icon Name="savings" />
        </div>
        <h3>Cloud cost savings</h3>
        <p>A review of your AWS or Azure bill and the architecture behind it: idle and oversized resources, servers doing work that serverless does for pennies, and the slow queries and missing caches that inflate compute.</p>
        <p>You get the savings quantified and ranked by payoff against effort, in a plan your team can act on itself or hand to me. This site runs on S3, CloudFront, Lambda, and DynamoDB for a few dollars a month.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card service-card">
        <div class="service-icon" aria-hidden="true">
          <Icon Name="audit" />
        </div>
        <h3>Audits for developers</h3>
        <p>A second set of senior eyes on your codebase: architecture and code review, a SQL Server performance pass over execution plans, indexes, and slow stored procedures, or a pre-release security check.</p>
        <p>You get a written report with findings ranked by impact and concrete next steps, not a list of lint warnings.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="button-row section-cta">
      <a class="button button-primary" href="#contact" @onclick="@(() => TopicState.Select("Freelance"))">Start a conversation</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
